post_install() {
    cat <<'EOT'
Shisa is installed. Set the application-lock password first. Only root can
set it, and it is separate from the login password:

  sudo passwd shisa-lock

Protected applications cannot authenticate until it is set. The password is
only used by Shisa's polkit dialog. It does not change the login password and
gives shisa-lock no login or sudo access.

Then enable the services:

  sudo systemctl enable --now shisad.service
  systemctl --user enable --now shisa-agent.service

Per-user protected applications live in /etc/shisa/users/<username>.toml.
You can also edit them in shisa-settings.
EOT
}

post_upgrade() {
    if [ -e /etc/pam.d/shisa.pacsave ]; then
        cat <<'EOT'
/etc/pam.d/shisa is no longer used by Shisa, authentication now goes through
polkit. Your local changes are kept in /etc/pam.d/shisa.pacsave. Remove that
file once you no longer need it.
EOT
    fi
    if ! getent passwd shisa-lock >/dev/null || passwd -S shisa-lock 2>/dev/null | grep -q ' L '; then
        cat <<'EOT'
The application-lock password is not set yet:

  sudo passwd shisa-lock

Protected applications cannot authenticate until it is set.
EOT
    fi
    cat <<'EOT'
Services keep running the old binaries after an upgrade. Restart them to load
the new version:

  sudo systemctl restart shisad.service
  systemctl --user restart shisa-agent.service
EOT
}

pre_remove() {
    cat <<'EOT'
You can stop the services before removing:

  sudo systemctl disable --now shisad.service
  systemctl --user disable --now shisa-agent.service

Configuration under /etc/shisa/ is kept.
The shisa-lock account is not removed automatically. To delete it:

  sudo userdel shisa-lock
EOT
}
